The scoped item graph is acyclic. Levels are 0 for the random-measure definition, compact-exhaustion lemma and scalar Poisson definition; 1 for the mean-measure, count-law normalization, count-fidi law and deletion lemmas; 2 for the Poisson/Cox definition; 3 for finite-intensity existence and Poisson-integral moments; 4 for finite Mecke and general existence; 5 for Campbell/Laplace/Mecke/Cox formulas. The finite-window B example is level 4. The batch-level scheduler metadata remains level 1 / prerequisite batch 30; item levels are separately computed from actual in-batch dependencies.

The local count-fidi lemma is needed to justify uniqueness of point-process law from evaluation cylinders; the finite-intensity Mecke lemma is needed before extending Mecke over the countable cell partition; the Poisson random-integral moment lemma supplies the conditional Cox second-moment calculation. These are A-page suppliers, with complete local proofs and explicit dependencies.

## Mathematical scope and conventions

Configurations are locally finite counting measures on a locally compact, second-countable Hausdorff space, with multiplicities permitted. Counts on infinite-intensity sets use the extended convention (+\infty) almost surely. The expected-measure lemma proves countable additivity and the expectation/integral interchange for arbitrary nonnegative Borel functions. The finite construction uses a Poisson total and product-distributed point locations; sigma-finite existence uses disjoint cells from a compact exhaustion and an explicit product-space axiom branch.

Sigma-finite means a countable measurable cover by sets of finite measure; on this second-countable locally compact space, every locally finite measure is sigma-finite by the compact exhaustion. Independence of counts is the independent-random-elements convention for their generated sigma-algebras.

Campbell, Laplace, add-one Mecke, its equivalent one-occurrence deletion form, the Mecke converse, Cox mean/Laplace/moment formulas, and the finite-window variance contrast are proved. The second-moment identity is stated with extended values; finite second moment is equivalent to ∫ f² dν < ∞ and E[ξ(f)²] < ∞, which then implies the needed first-moment finiteness. Multiplicity is preserved when deleting one point. No compensator result is used.

